<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · c. 793–1066 (the Viking Age)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Kings to Thralls</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship.jpg" alt="Oseberg ship"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship">Oseberg ship</a><span class="soc-credit">Petter Ulleland · CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, from about 793 to 1066, the deepest line ran between the free and the unfree. The Eddic poem Rígsþula, of uncertain date and preserved in a 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, tells how the god Ríg fathered three sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">Thrall</a>, Karl and Jarl, the ancestors of slaves, free farmers and nobles, and archaeology confirms a society divided along broadly those lines. Free men owned weapons and met at assemblies called things to make law and settle disputes. The free farmers were the great majority. Above them stood chieftains, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarls</a> and kings, whose many small realms grew during the period into the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Below them were freed slaves and the thralls, many of them captives taken on raids. Birth mattered, but wealth won by raiding, trade and service abroad could raise a family, and capture or debt could make a free person a slave.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> c. 793–1066 (the Viking Age)</span><span><b>People</b> c. 1–2 million (est.)</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 7</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Konungar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Kings)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">A handful</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:37%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Jarlar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Jarls)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">Fewer than 1 in 1,000 (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:49%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Hersar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Chieftains)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.3–1% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:62%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Húskarlar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Retainers)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–1.5% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:75%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:81%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Bœndr</span> <span class="soc-en">(Free farmers)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 81% (the rest, est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:87%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Leysingjar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Freed slaves)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–3% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:6"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:15%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Þrælar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Thralls)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 10–20% (est.)</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/harald-bluetooth.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>A handful</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Konungar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Kings)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">konungr</p><p class="soc-kid">Viking kings led armies and fleets, gave gifts to their warriors and in time made their people Christian.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Kingship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> began small: around 800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> alone had some 30 petty kingdoms, and kings’ names are reliably known only for the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>. A king (konungr) was above all a war leader who held power by winning followers, rewarding them with gifts and leading them to plunder and conquest. Kings were acclaimed at the assemblies of free men, and the early kings had little power to compel: they led largely by consent. Royal power grew over the period. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorm-the-old">Gorm the Old</a> founded a lasting dynasty, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a> raised the great runestone at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a>, which claims that he won all Denmark and Norway and made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> Christian. Kings kept armed retinues, called out the ship levy (leiðangr) and spread the new faith, sometimes by force, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Denmark and Norway together. No throne was safe: Harald Bluetooth was overthrown by his own son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a> was killed in battle in 1000.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Kings came from royal families, but no fixed rule of succession existed: sons and kinsmen competed, and the throne went to whoever could win over the chieftains and the assemblies and win the battles.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> A few royal families: around 800 Norway alone had some 30 small kingdoms, and by the end of the period three kingdoms, Denmark, Norway and Sweden, were taking shape.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olof-skotkonung">Olof Skötkonung</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>Fewer than 1 in 1,000 (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Jarlar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Jarls)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">jarl</p><p class="soc-kid">Jarls were powerful lords, one step below the king, who owned great farms and ruled whole regions.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarl</a> (earl) was the greatest lord below a king. The title could mark an independent ruler or a king’s deputy. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> the jarls of Lade ruled Trøndelag and Hålogaland with as much power as many kings, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakon-sigurdsson">Håkon Sigurdsson</a> (Haakon Jarl) governed most of Norway from about 975 to 995, at first under the Danish king. The earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-isles">Northern Isles</a> under the Norwegian crown, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thorfinn-the-mighty">Thorfinn the Mighty</a> in the 11th century. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> the jarl was the king’s deputy, as Ulf Jarl was to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a>, and when Cnut conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> he made Eric of Lade earl of Northumbria. Jarls owned great estates with large halls, horses and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thralls</a>, kept their own armed followers and showed their rank in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> clothing, brooches and arm rings. Rígsþula pictures Jarl as a warrior who rides, hunts with hawks and hounds, and wins eighteen estates by war.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The title passed within great families such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarls</a> of Lade and the earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, or was granted by a king to a deputy; a jarl who lost a struggle with a king could lose everything, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakon-sigurdsson">Håkon Sigurdsson</a> did when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 995.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the title belonged to a few great families and to the deputies of kings.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakon-sigurdsson">Håkon Sigurdsson</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thorfinn-the-mighty">Thorfinn the Mighty</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">Jarl</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: The stern of the Gokstad ship, the burial ship of a great lord in Vestfold, c. 900 · Colgill · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gokstad-ship---Stern-showin.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-3.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>c. 0.3–1% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Hersar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Chieftains)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">hersir, goði, höfðingi</p><p class="soc-kid">Chieftains led the farmers of their district in war and at the assemblies where laws were made.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarls</a> stood the chieftains of districts, wealthy landowners who led their neighbours in war and law. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> a hersir commanded the men of his district and owed loyalty to a jarl or king; the later sagas give each jarl four or more hersar. Iceland, settled from about 870 and without a king, was led by its goðar: there were 36 chieftaincies (goðorð), later 39, which could be inherited, bought or shared. Every free farmer of means had to follow a goði of his choice, who backed him in lawsuits and could call on him for armed support, and the goðar ran the local assemblies, sat in the law council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a> and named its judges. Pagan worship had no separate order of priests: chieftains kept the halls where the communal sacrifices and feasts were held, and three runestones on the Danish island of Funen name local men as goði. From the early 11th century Norwegian kings drew chieftains into their service as “landed men” (lendir menn).</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Chieftaincy passed mostly within families, but land, followers and success in feuds and raids could make a rich farmer a chieftain, and in Iceland a goðorð could simply be bought.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the hersar, goðar and other chieftains with their households; Iceland, the only land with a fixed number, recognised 36 chieftaincies (goðorð), later 39.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thorgeir-ljosvetningagodi">Þorgeir Ljósvetningagoði</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: The Gjermundbu helmet, from the grave of a warrior of rank in Norway, 10th century · NTNU Vitenskapsmuseet · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gjermundbu_helmet_-_cropped.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-4"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/varangian-guard.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 0.5–1.5% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Húskarlar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Retainers)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">lið, drengir</p><p class="soc-kid">Kings and chieftains kept bands of warriors who lived with them and were rewarded with gifts.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarls</a> and chieftains kept armed households of free men, called lið and in later sources hirð, who lived with their lord, fought at his side and were rewarded with gifts and pay. Such a retainer was a housecarl (húskarl) or a drengr, a “warrior”; the skald Sigvatr Þórðarson called the retainers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf II of Norway</a> heiðþegar, “receivers of pay”. They were bound to their lord and expected to avenge him if he was killed. The best equipped had a helmet, a mail shirt and a sword, marks of wealth beyond most farmers. Many served far from home. The Turinge runestone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> remembers Þorsteinn, who fell “in the east in Garðar” (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>) as commander of a retinue. So many Swedes joined the Byzantine emperor’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>, formed in the late 10th century, that a law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastergotland">Västergötland</a> barred anyone staying in “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>” from inheriting, and Cnut’s conquest brought a royal guard of housecarls to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, which fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> in 1066.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Free men skilled in arms joined a lord’s following by his favour, and loyal service was rewarded with gifts; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> the king’s men could rise to be his landed men (lendir menn), the highest rank in the royal household.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: every king, jarl and chieftain kept his own armed household, from a few men to several hundred.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-5.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>About 81% (the rest, est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Bœndr</span> <span class="soc-en">(Free farmers)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">bóndi (pl. bœndr), karl</p><p class="soc-kid">Most Vikings were farmers, not raiders: they grew grain, kept animals and met at assemblies to make laws.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Most free Scandinavians were farmers (bœndr), the karlar of Rígsþula, who worked their own land with their households. A farm centred on a longhouse; its people grew barley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">rye</a>, oats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a>, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and pigs, fished, hunted and made woollen cloth. Wealth ranged from great farms with many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thralls</a> to small farms with one or two, or none. A free man was expected to own weapons, typically a spear, a shield and a knife, and to answer the call to war, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> through the ship levy (leiðangr), first recorded in the late 10th century. He had a voice at the thing, the assembly of free men of full age, which made law, judged disputes and acclaimed kings. Few farmers were ever raiders: others sailed as traders or settled in Iceland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> and the lands to the east. In trading towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birka">Birka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaupang">Kaupang</a> lived craftsmen and merchants, and free but landless servants (húskarlar) worked on other men’s farms.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Sons inherited family land, cleared new land or won wealth abroad; debt, crime or capture in war could reduce a free person to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thrall</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other tiers: the free majority of farmers, fishers, craftsmen and traders with their households. The other ranks are estimates, so this share is one too.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulating">Gulating</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frostating">Frostating</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Þjóðveldisbærinn, a reconstruction of the farmhouse excavated at Stöng, Iceland · Thomas Ormston ( Ormstont ) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St%C3%B6ng_Viking_Longhouse.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-6"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>c. 1–3% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Leysingjar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Freed slaves)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">leysingi (pl. leysingjar)</p><p class="soc-kid">Some thralls were set free, and some were even given land to farm by their former owners.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thrall</a> could be freed by the owner, in the owner’s will, or by buying freedom with money earned; according to the sagas the Norwegian magnate Erling Skjalgsson set each of his thralls a price and let them earn it by working for themselves. The freed slave (leysingi) was free but not equal. He still owed loyalty to his former master and had to support him at the assembly; if he died without children, the former master inherited his land and goods; and the penalty for killing a freed slave was lower than for a freeborn man. Only after at least two generations did a family become fully free. Freedom often came with land to farm: Aud the Deep-Minded, who settled in Iceland in the late 9th century, freed the thralls she brought with her and gave them land, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landnamabok">Landnámabók</a>. Most of these rules are known from laws written down in the 12th and 13th centuries.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Freedom came only by the owner’s choice, by will or by purchase; the freed slave’s grandchildren could expect to count as fully free.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: freed slaves, who often stayed on as dependants of their former owners.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">Freedmen</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Erling Skjalgsson sets his thralls to day-work so that they can buy their freedom: Erik Werenskiold's illustration for Snorri's sagas, 1899 · Erik Werenskiold · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erik_Werenskiold_-_Erling_set_his_Thralls_to_Daywork_-_NG.K%26H.B.05040_-_National_Museum_of_Art%2C_Architecture_and_Design_(cropped).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>c. 10–20% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Þrælar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Thralls)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">þræll, ambátt</p><p class="soc-kid">Thralls were enslaved people, often captured in raids, who were owned by others and made to work without pay.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">Thralls</a> were property: they could be bought, sold and punished, and the 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulating">Gulating</a> law made anyone who harmed a thrall pay the owner, as for damage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. People became thralls by birth to thralls, through debt or as a punishment, and above all through capture: raiders carried off monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> in 793 and, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> say, “a great number of women” from an Irish village in 821. Most captives were sold on, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and the eastern rivers, to the Islamic world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. Those kept at home herded animals, fished, cut wood, built fences, cooked and milked; some women were forced to become their owners’ concubines, and some trusted thralls managed farms. Excavations suggest that thralls were sometimes killed to be buried with their owners. Some kept their faith, like the captive nun at Hedeby whose psalms moved Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimbert">Rimbert</a> to give his horse for her freedom; others ran away or saved to buy their freedom.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Freedom came only through the owner: as a gift, in a will, or by purchase with money the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thrall</a> had earned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> faded between the 12th and early 14th centuries, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> became Christian.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: popular accounts put thralls at up to a quarter of the population; most households of means kept a few.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">Thrall</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Ái and Edda, forebears of the thralls in the Eddic poem Rígsþula, welcome the god Ríg: illustration by W. G. Collingwood, 1908 · W. G. Collingwood · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rig_in_Great-grandfather's_Cottage.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="vikings">Be born in Viking Age Scandinavia</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> society was more open than the fixed orders of Rígsþula suggest. Raiding, trade and service abroad brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> to men of modest birth, and a leader who won followers could become a chieftain; settlers in Iceland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> claimed new land for themselves. Fortune turned fast at the top. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a> won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 995 with wealth gained as a Viking leader in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and lost it with his life at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-svolder">Battle of Svolder</a> in 1000, defeated by the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and by Eric of Lade, the son of Håkon Jarl, who had been killed as Olaf seized power. Lower down, people fell as well as rose: debt, crime or capture turned free people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thralls</a>, while freed slaves rose only slowly, their families counting as fully free after two generations. In the 11th century the ranks began to set more firmly, as kings turned chieftains into royal officials and the Church, which held that Christians should not own fellow Christians, undercut the slave trade.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>Free women took the rank of their fathers and husbands, but law and custom gave them more room than women in most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The housewife (húsfreyja) had authority over the indoor household and managed the farm’s stores and business; the keys found in many women’s graves are usually read as a sign of that role. Families arranged marriages, but a bride brought her own property, a wife could divorce and remarry, and widows and unmarried women over twenty were legally independent. Women inherited land: the 11th-century Hillersjö runestone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppland">Uppland</a> tells how Gerlög inherited the estate of her daughter Inga. Women also raised runestones, and some were priestesses (gyðjur) or seeresses, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship">Oseberg ship</a> held two women buried with great wealth. A 2017 DNA study showed that the weapon grave Bj 581 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birka">Birka</a> was a woman’s, but whether she fought is debated, and the shieldmaidens of the sagas are literature. Enslaved women had none of these rights.</p></section>
