<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Brink, S. and Price, N. (eds) (2008) The Viking World. London: Routledge.</li><li>Jesch, J. (1991) Women in the Viking Age. Woodbridge: Boydell Press.</li><li>Karras, R. M. (1988) Slavery and Society in Medieval Scandinavia. New Haven: Yale University Press.</li><li>Price, N. (2020) Children of Ash and Elm: A History of the Vikings. London: Allen Lane.</li><li>Sawyer, B. (2000) The Viking-Age Rune-Stones: Custom and Commemoration in Early Medieval Scandinavia. Oxford: Oxford University Press.</li><li>Winroth, A. (2014) The Age of the Vikings. Princeton: Princeton University Press.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: Estimate: Scandinavia in the 10th century: Denmark perhaps half a million or more, Sweden and Norway somewhat less together; Germany and Scandinavia together held about 4 million in 1000 (drawn from J. C. Russell and D. Herlihy).</p></section>
